  "synthesis_conclusions": [
    "The review explored the influence of the built environment on human experience using approaches from neuroscience by systematically examining existing literature.",
    "The review contributed to establishing a general conceptual framework for conducting interdisciplinary studies in neuroscience and architecture.",
    "Advances in science and technology (psychophysiological measurements, neuroimaging) enable a multidimensional, holistic, and comprehensive exploration of human experience in the built environment.",
    "Developing and emerging concepts help understand humans' behavioral, sensational, emotional, and cognitive responses to the built environment with a high degree of objectivity.",
    "Most concepts in the field are developed in theoretical terms, but experimental studies have not yet succeeded in verifying most of these theories.",
    "Neuroscientific knowledge can provide evidence-based and objective data, addressing a major shortcoming of current theories in examining human experience.",
    "Developments in neuroscientific knowledge will play an increasingly important role in understanding human needs, responses, and experiences in built environments, and in directing future architecture and built environment studies."
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "Several theoretical concepts (thigmotaxis, pareidolia, proxemics, proprioception, mnemonics) have not yet been investigated in experimental studies.",
    "Difficulty in formulating theoretical concepts as testable hypotheses for experimental studies.",
    "Challenges in measuring data in experimental studies.",
    "Experimental findings remain isolated, and further experiments face challenges in measuring and associating data.",
    "A large array of concepts requires further research."
  ],
